steps.reasoning_content:
No.

PNG specific parser vulnerabilities often in `PngChunk::parseTXTChunk`:
- zlib compressed `zTXt`/`iTXt`, decompress with output size calculation. We can craft zlib bombs/truncated streams. If decompressed length enormous, potential overflow.
- PNG chunk payload begins keyword null. For iTXt: keyword NUL, compression flag, compression method, language NUL, translated keyword NUL, text.
- Exiv2 recognizes XMP in iTXt and text in tEXt/zTXt.
Known bug in `PngChunk::decodeTXTChunk` from 2023:
```
static void decodeTXTChunk(Image* png, const ChunkData& chunk, TxtChunkType type)
{
  size_t textStart = ...;
  DataBuf text = chunk.pData;
  ...
  if (type == zTXt_Chunk) {
    zlibUncompress(text.pData_ + textStart, text.size_ - textStart, decompressed);
  }
}
```
If keyword has no NUL, `find` returns npos, then arithmetic wraps causing huge size. Fix likely checks.
Craft zTXt/tEXt/iTXt payload without NUL and compressed data. Test.

